{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-138881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2t$ meters per second.\nFind an expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2t) \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡t+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =2\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =2{sec}^{2}⁡t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138881" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de"} {"question" "q-138881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+4"] ["math" "2{sec}^{2}⁡t+4"] ["math" "2{sec}^{2}⁡t-2"] ["math" "4\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+2"] ["math" "{sec}^{2}⁡t-4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138881" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2{sec}^{2}⁡t+4"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $9\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{2t})$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡({e}^{2t})] \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t})\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t})\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =-6{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =9\\cdot [-6{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t})] \\\\  & =-54{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({e}^{2t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345208" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de"} {"question" "q-345208" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345208" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-54e^{2t}\\sin(e^{2t})"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t$ meters per second. \n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sec}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =ma(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(\\frac{\\pi }{4})|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345223" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de"} {"question" "q-345223" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345223" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $6\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}$ meters per second.\nFind an expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =6\\cdot (3{t}^{2}-2t) \\\\  & =18{t}^{2}-12t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138882" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de"} {"question" "q-138882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12{t}^{2}+4t"] ["math" "18t+12"] ["math" "18{t}^{2}-12t"] ["math" "3{t}^{2}-2t"] ["math" "9t-6"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138882" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "18{t}^{2}-12t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\text{kg}$ moves along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=\\frac{2t}{t+1}$ meters per second.  Find an expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2t}{t+1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{(t+1)\\cdot (2t)^{′}-2t\\cdot (t+1)^{′}}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t+1)-2t(1)}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2t+2)-2t}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{(t+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(t+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de"} {"question" "q-345209" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345209" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{1}{(t+1)^2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $4\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=2{t}^{2}+{e}^{5-t}$ meters.\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=5$ seconds." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+{e}^{5-t}) \\\\  & =4t-{e}^{5-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t-{e}^{5-t}) \\\\  & =4-(-{e}^{5-t}) \\\\  & =4+{e}^{5-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=5\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =4+{e}^{5-5} \\\\  & =4+1 \\\\  & =5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =4\\cdot (5) \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F|=|20|=20\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138907" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de"} {"question" "q-138907" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138907" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "10\\,\\text{N}"] ["math" "12\\,\\text{N}"] ["math" "20\\,\\text{N}"] ["math" "5\\,\\text{N}"] ["math" "8\\,\\text{N}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138907" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "20\\,\\text{N}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $3\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}-{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}-3{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}-6t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =3\\cdot (4{e}^{2t}-6t) \\\\  & =12{e}^{2t}-18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "12{e}^{2t}-18t"] ["math" "12{e}^{2t}-6t"] ["math" "18{e}^{2t}-12t"] ["math" "24{e}^{2t}-12t"] ["math" "4{e}^{2t}-6t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138901" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "12{e}^{2t}-18t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons $(\\text{N}).$  At time $t>0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={e}^{2t}$ meters.\n\nCalculate the magnitude of the force at time $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}$ seconds. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe magnitude of the force equals\n{{field-1}} $\\text{N}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{2t}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2{e}^{2t} \\\\  & =4{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the acceleration at $t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{s}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) & =4{e}^{2\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}]} \\\\  & =4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) & =ma(\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the magnitude of the force is\n\n$$\n|F(2)|=|2|=2\\,\\text{N}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345345" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de"} {"question" "q-345345" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345345" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $1\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.  At time $t$ seconds, the position of the particle is given by $s(t)=t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ meters.  Find the expression for the force acting on the particle at time $t.$\n\n$F(t)=$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "By Newton’s second law,\n\n$$\nF(t)=ma(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $F(t)$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a(t)$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force, in Newtons, is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =ma(t) \\\\  & =1\\cdot (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-345339" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2\\cos(t)-t\\sin(t)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)=t-4\\sqrt{t}$ meters.\nFind the expression for the force acting on the body at time $t.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration.\n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4\\sqrt{t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-4{t}^{1/2}) \\\\  & =1-4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {t}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =1-2{t}^{-1/2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2{t}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =0-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot {t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & ={t}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t\\sqrt{t}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the force in Newtons is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F & =ma \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{t\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de"} {"question" "q-138902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1+\\frac{5}{t\\sqrt{t}}"] ["math" "1+t\\sqrt{t}"] ["math" "5t\\sqrt{t}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{t\\sqrt{t}}"] ["math" "\\frac{5}{t\\sqrt{t}}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{5}{t\\sqrt{t}}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $2\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the velocity of the body is given by $v(t)=2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2$ meters per second.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2}-36t+2) \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}-6t-36\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{t}^{2}-6t-36 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}-t-6 & =0 \\\\ (t-3)(t+2) & =0 \\\\ t & =-2,\\,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\ge 0,$ we disregard the negative solution. \n\nTherefore, the force has zero magnitude at $t=3\\,\\text{s}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "f7ad419d93ee9149ffc25caab33e9435e5bfce01f2e6915a31407868c03eb4de"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t=1\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=2\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=3\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=5\\,\\text{s}"] ["math" "t=6\\,\\text{s}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138769" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t=3\\,\\text{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "A body of mass $5\\,\\text{kg}$ is moving in a straight line along the $x$-axis under the action of a single force $F,$ measured in Newtons.\nAt time $t\\ge 0$ seconds, the position of the body is given by $s(t)={t}^{4}-150{t}^{2}+8$ meters.\nCalculate the time at which the force acting on the body has zero magnitude." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14033858/state.json" "file_sha256" "e5b347a0a7cb7316b86fb9aea2ef3ba0c1de22fae6af8da10da4fb28f670827b"} {"question" "q-138916" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to Newton’s second law\n\n$$\nF=ma,\n$$\n\n \nwhere $F$ is the force, $m$ is the mass, and $a$ is the acceleration. \n\nTo find the velocity $v(t),$ we differentiate the displacement $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-150{t}^{2}+8) \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-300t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration $a(t),$ we differentiate the velocity $v(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-300t) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-300\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the mass is always positive, the force has zero magnitude when the acceleration equals zero. So, we set $a(t)=0$ and solve for $t:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}12{t}^{2}-300 & =0 \\\\ 12{t}^{2} & =300 \\\\ {t}^{2} 